  <div class="container-md mb-5">
    <h1>Why not Anki?</h1>

    <div class="mb-5 small bg-light rounded p-4 fst-italic">
      <p class="mb-2">Disclaimer: I’m not trying to change the minds of Anki users — they are some of the most dedicated learners I’ve ever encountered.</p>
      <p class="mb-0">This article explains why Anki doesn’t meet <i class="fst-normal">my</i> language-learning needs.</p>
    </div>

    <p>People sometimes ask me why I don’t just use Anki (or Quizlet, or Memrise).</p>

    <p>I created Vocably because none of the existing solutions let me create flashcards quickly enough.</p>

    <blockquote class="call-out">
      It takes 12 steps to add a new word to Anki.
    </blockquote>

    <p>Here they are:</p>

    <ol>
      <li>See a new word on any website.</li>
      <li>Select and translate it with Google Translate (or another tool).</li>
      <li>Copy the word.</li>
      <li>Open Anki/Quizlet/Memrise.</li>
      <li>Click “Add new card.”</li>
      <li>Paste the word.</li>
      <li>Return to the website.</li>
      <li>Copy the translation.</li>
      <li>Go back to Anki/Quizlet/Memrise.</li>
      <li>Paste the translation.</li>
      <li>Click “Save.”</li>
      <li>Switch back to the website.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>If you’re watching a YouTube video, there are even more steps, because you also have to:</p>

    <ol>
      <li>Open Google Translate.</li>
      <li>Type the new word manually.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>And if you want to store words in their base form instead of as plurals or past-tense verbs (“acquire” rather than “acquired”), you have to edit them by hand — which means even more typing and looking things up.</p>

    <blockquote class="call-out">
      Adding a new word to Vocably is just 3 steps.
    </blockquote>

    <ol>
      <li>See a new word on a website or in a YouTube video.</li>
      <li>Select it and translate with Vocably.</li>
      <li>Save the card — it already includes definitions, parts of speech, translations, usage examples, and the word in its base form.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>3 steps versus 12.</p>

    <p>So, to answer the question <i>Why not Anki?</i>:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Fewer steps to add a new word.</li>
      <li>No app switching — no losing focus while reading or watching.</li>
    </ul>

